1. e4 { -KEY- } e5 { The starting point of this repertoire. From the very beginning of chess history 1. e4 e5 has been established as the most principled and sound way to face White's most popular first move. Also, learning the open games is the key to understanding chess. Active play, concrete fight for the centre and maneuvering in flexible structures are exactly what strong players should excel at. } 2. Nf3 { The most logical move and by far the most popular. White develops the knight attacking e5 and once he develops the light squared bishop he is ready to castle. It will be seen in 23 theoretical chapters (out of 29). } Nc6 3. Bb5 { The Spanish Opening, also called Ruy Lopez, will be the topic of first 8 theoretical chapters. } Nf6 ( { The more popular } 3... a6 { is another story. There are a lot of completely different variations in the Spanish/Ruy Lopez making this opening very rich in ideas. } ) 
4. O-O { Allowing Black to take on e4 is the main move. } ( { There is also } 4. d3 { which is called the Anti-Berlin and } 4. Nc3 { transposes to the Four Knights Game with 4.Bb5. } ) 
Nxe4 { Taking the pawn might look a bit risky at first, White should easily regain it. However, Black will easily catch up with the development in the meantime. } 5. Re1 ( { This move has a very drawish reputation, and rightly so. Black faces few theoretical problems. That's the only big alternative to } 5. d4 { . } ) 
Nd6 6. Nxe5 Be7 7. Bf1 ( { The most popular and best move. According to classical principles White shouldn't block the d-pawn with his bishop. We will also cover } 7. Bd3 { but if Black doesn't fall for any cheap tricks it's not dangerous. } ) 
Nxe5 ( { I recommend not to allow } 7... O-O 8. Nf3 ) 
 ( { or } 7... Nf5 8. Nf3 { , because with more pieces on the board White has better chances to acquire a slight edge. } ) 
8. Rxe5 O-O 9. d4 Bf6 10. Re1 Re8 ( { A simple and logical move but the more popular } 10... Nf5 11. c3 d5 { is not bad either. It's just a question of taste. } ) 
11. c3 { There are a lot of different moves at this point but let's see the most popular one. } Rxe1 12. Qxe1 Ne8 { Just preparing ...d5. } 13. Bf4 d5 ( { Note that this move wouldn't be possible if we played } 12... Nf5 { because after } 13. Bf4 d5 { White would have } 14. Bxc7 $1 ) 
14. Bd3 g6 { The position resembles the exchange French and Black would like to trade light squared bishops with ... Ng7 and ...Bf5. } 15. Nd2 Ng7 16. Nf3 Bf5 { Black equalizes. } 
