1. e4 e5 { The starting point of this repertoire. From the very beginning of chess history 1. e4 e5 has been established as the most principled and sound way to face White's most popular first move. Also, learning the open games is the key to understanding chess. Active play, concrete fight for the centre and maneuvering in flexible structures are exactly what strong players should excel at. } 2. Nf3 { The most logical move and by far the most popular. White develops the knight attacking e5 and once he develops the light squared bishop he is ready to castle. It will be seen in 23 theoretical chapters (out of 29). } Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 { This is called Four Knights Game - I guess you can see why :) Now White has a lot of different moves but the main move is 4.Bb5 and 4.d4 is the Scotch variation and 4.Bc4 is the Italian variation. } 4. Bb5 Nd4 { The recent correspondence and over the board praxis has established this move to be very promising, yielding Black great results. As we will see, White can easily end up in trouble if he does not proceed cautiously. } 5. Ba4 { -KEY- } c6 { A little but very powerful move. Black may play some ...b5, ...a5 chasing the bishop but the main point is revealed when White accepts the gambit and takes on e5. } ( { The two times more popular } 5... Bc5 { is playable but } 6. Nxe5 O-O 7. Nd3 Bb6 8. e5 Ne8 9. Nd5 { doesn't seem to be so convincing for Black. } ) 
6. Nxe5 { Of course, the only serious way to try and justify the loss of tempo with Bb5-a4 is to take the pawn. However, Black can now develop a powerful initiative with accurate play. } ( 6. O-O a5 { is easy. } ) 
d6 { Somehow having ...Bg4 on the cards is extremely important now and this move wouldn't be possible if ...c6 wouldn't block the a4-e8 diagonal. As we are going to see, Black has a very pleasant compensation. } 7. Nf3 Bg4 8. d3 Bh5 $1 ( { A cunning move, which is yet to be tested in over-the-board play. So far only } 8... d5 { and } Nd7 { have been played and Simon Williams mentions only these moves on his ChessBase DVD 'Rocket Repertoire: The Four Knights'. Black's basic idea is ...Nxf3+, when after the recapture the bishop would need to retreat to h5 anyway. By playing ...Bh5 first, he maintains a high degree of flexibility and 'passes the ball to' White, for whom it is not easy to find a useful move. } ( { One example of the usefulness of the bishop on h5 is the variation } 8... b5 9. Be3 $1 Nxf3 10. gxf3 { and the bishop is hanging. } ) 
 ) 
9. h3 Nxf3+ 10. gxf3 d5 $11 { Not the only, but the most ambitious move. Black opens the position to unlock the full potential of his pieces. } 
