1. e4 e5 { The starting point of this repertoire. From the very beginning of chess history 1. e4 e5 has been established as the most principled and sound way to face White's most popular first move. Also, learning the open games is the key to understanding chess. Active play, concrete fight for the centre and maneuvering in flexible structures are exactly what strong players should excel at. } 2. Nf3 { The most logical move and by far the most popular. White develops the knight attacking e5 and once he develops the light squared bishop he is ready to castle. It will be seen in 23 theoretical chapters (out of 29). } Nc6 3. Bc4 { The Italian game is one of the oldest openings in chess. It was developed in the 16th and 17th centuries by famous Italian masters Damiano, Polerio and Greco. It has always been frequently seen in beginners' and amateurs' games but its popularity among strong players gradually ceased in favor of the Ruy Lopez, which was considered a better try for an advantage until very recently. However, things have changed dramatically in recent years. Due to the futility of their attempts against the Berlin Defence, top players have started seeking ways to pose Black problems in the long-forgotten Italian. As a result of the trend set by Carlsen, Anand, Caruana, Karjakin, Vachier- Lagrave, Giri and other strong players it has suddenly become more popular than the Ruy Lopez and its theory has grown enormously. Unlike just a few years ago, Black is nowadays required to know the nuances of different lines and move orders to obtain a good position out of the opening. We have 4 theoretical chapters covering the Italian and there are also some gambit tries possible from here. -KEY- } Bc5 ( 3... Nf6 { is fine but it's a completely different story. There is for example } 4. Ng5 d5 5. exd5 Na5 6. Bb5 c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. Be2 { with very complex play. } ) 
4. b4 { The Evans gambit essentially belongs to the 19th century, but White players still try it occasionally, hoping to surprise Black, so it is important to be prepared. I found a great way to obtain a promising position without having to remember a lot of lines. } Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. d4 b5 $5 { I believe this is a great practical choice. Black not only effortlessly achieves at least an equality, he also has a chance of throwing White completely off preparation, due to the rareness of this move. All that at a cost of returning the sacrificed pawn, which Black does in many other lines anyway. This move hasn't been considered in any source known to us. } 7. Bxb5 Nxd4 { The point. This move would not be possible with the bishop on c4, due to Nxe5. } 8. Nxd4 exd4 9. Qxd4 Qf6 ( 9... Nf6 10. Ba3 { would be White's dream. } ) 
10. O-O Bb6 11. Qd3 { Clearly the endgame wasn't encouraging. } Ne7 { Black has no problems. } 
