1. e4 e5 { The starting point of this repertoire. From the very beginning of chess history 1. e4 e5 has been established as the most principled and sound way to face White's most popular first move. Also, learning the open games is the key to understanding chess. Active play, concrete fight for the centre and maneuvering in flexible structures are exactly what strong players should excel at. } 2. d4 ( { This will be the main topic of the final theoretical chapter. We will also cover some funky stuff like going for the Scholar's Mate with } 2. Qh5 { or the Bongcloud Attack with } 2. Ke2 ) 
 { -KEY- } exd4 3. Qxd4 { This is called the Centre Game. White loses an important tempo in the opening and develops his pieces awkwardly. Black can successfully fight for more than equality. } ( { There is also } 3. c3 { (Danish Gambit) which we will not accept and meet with } d5 { . } ) 
Nc6 4. Qe3 ( { There is also } 4. Qc4 { covered in the theoretical section. We should just complete quickly our development one way or the other. } ) 
Nf6 5. Nc3 Bb4 { Just developing actively. } 6. Bd2 O-O 7. O-O-O { That was White's idea. He wants to attack us on the kingside by pawnstorming us with f3, g4, h4 etc. That might be very dangerous but he isn't too well developed. } Re8 { White already feels the heat on e4, there is also ... d5 in the air. White usually sacrifices the pawn. } 8. Qg3 { White gets out of the pin and if he manages to play f3 his position is actually quite nice. We have to accept the sacrifice. } Nxe4 9. Nxe4 Rxe4 { White may have some compensation but it won't be enough for a full pawn. } 
